      "claim": "Bayesian inference starts with a prior distribution reflecting existing knowledge about theta",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:32:53\u201300:33:37] 'We start from prior distribution that reflects our knowledge, our preference, and possible values of theta.'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Bayesian parameter estimation produces a posterior distribution P(theta | X1,...,Xn) via Bayes' theorem",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:33:37\u201300:34:13] 'we simply derive a posterior distribution, P of theta given X1, Xn, by applying Bayes' theorem.'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "The posterior distribution is derived by multiplying the likelihood (product of P(Xi | theta)) with the prior and renormalizing",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:33:37\u201300:34:13] 'So like if a function looks like this, so this is just a product of P of Xi given theta, since all of our observations are independent and identically distributed, we multiply it by prior distribution and we renormalize it according to Bayes' theorem.'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Bayesian inference replaces point estimates with a full probability distribution over theta",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:34:14\u201300:34:56] 'Unlike maximum life estimation, our answer is no longer a point estimate, but a distribution.'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "The frequentist approach uses point estimates (e.g., maximum likelihood estimates)",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:32:13\u201300:32:53] 'an alternative to maximum value estimation would be Bayesian inference'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "The posterior distribution is a probability distribution over theta",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:34:14\u201300:34:56] 'our answer is no longer a point estimate, but a distribution'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "The likelihood function is the product of P(Xi | theta) for independent and identically distributed observations",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:33:37\u201300:34:13] 'this is just a product of P of Xi given theta, since all of our observations are independent and identically distributed'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "The term 'maximum life estimation' is a mishearing of 'maximum likelihood estimation'",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:32:13\u201300:32:53] 'an alternative to maximum value estimation would be Bayesian inference' (note: 'value' is likely a mishearing of 'likelihood')"
    }
  ],
